{
  "subtype": "faceted_decomposition",
  "concept": "airline_competitive_advantage",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "cabin_crew_attentiveness",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "Quality of in-flight crew interaction (friendly/attentive vs rude/dismissive) is an aspect OF an airline's service offering and thus a genuine part-of the competitive-advantage concept, not an external predictor of it."
    },
    {
      "name": "seat_comfort_legroom",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "Physical seat/cabin comfort and pitch is a constitutive dimension of the airline product experience, a legitimate meronymic facet of competitive advantage."
    },
    {
      "name": "customer_service_responsiveness",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "Responsiveness/helpfulness of service is an aspect OF the airline's offering, so it qualifies as a facet; however its underlying dimension (helpful vs dismissive service) substantially overlaps with cabin_crew_attentiveness."
    }
  ],
  "facet_columns": ["cabin_crew_attentiveness", "seat_comfort_legroom", "customer_service_responsiveness"],
  "non_facet_columns": [],
  "mutual_exclusivity": 0.5,
  "exhaustiveness": 0.4,
  "rationale": "All three added columns are true constitutive facets (parts-of) the concept of airline competitive advantage: they each describe an aspect of the airline's product/service rather than an external cause or predictor. However, MECE is only moderately satisfied. Mutual exclusivity is weakened because cabin_crew_attentiveness and customer_service_responsiveness both capture essentially the same underlying dimension -- attentive/helpful vs dismissive/unresponsive service -- with near-identical value polarities (friendly_attentive/proactive_helpful vs rude_dismissive/dismissive_unhelpful), so the concept's 'service' aspect is double-counted. Exhaustiveness is limited: the columns effectively cover only two distinct aspects (interpersonal/service quality and seat comfort), while other clearly constitutive facets of an airline's competitive advantage -- food & beverages, inflight entertainment, value for money, punctuality/reliability, route network, and check-in/boarding/baggage handling -- are absent, leaving major parts of the concept uncovered."
}

